        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe `getSelfLink()` logic is in the wrong unit, and it duplicates a path that already lives in the web layer.\n\n**1. API routing knowledge doesn't belong in the JPA entity.**\n`Owner` is a persistence/domain entity. Its existing `@Transient` getters (`getIdentityKey`, `getContactPreference`, `getAgeBand`) are all derivations over the owner's *own domain fields* \u2014 they're intrinsic to what an owner *is*. `getSelfLink()` is categorically different: it hardcodes a REST transport concern (the `/api/owners/` URL path) into the domain model. The entity now \"knows\" how the HTTP API is routed, which is a layering leak. A maintainer who later relocates or versions the API base path would not expect to have to edit a model class to do it.\n\n**2. It duplicates the path literal already owned by the controller.**\n`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:251-252` already builds this exact path idiomatically:\n```java\nheaders.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()\n    .path(\"/api/owners/{id}\").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());\n```\nThe new method reintroduces the same `/api/owners/` string in a second place (`Owner.java:492`). The two are now free to drift \u2014 the `Location` header and `selfLink` could diverge, which is exactly the kind of inconsistency the single-source rule exists to prevent.\n\n**Suggested direction:**\n- Keep the entity free of URL concerns. Compute `selfLink` in the web layer where the path already lives \u2014 e.g. set it on `OwnerDto` in the `ownerMapper`/mapping step (or in the controller), ideally sharing the same path constant/`UriComponentsBuilder` the `Location` header uses so there's one definition of `/api/owners/{id}`.\n- The `openapi.yml` addition is fine and stays as-is; only the Java placement needs to move."
